{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011CgTLdAfpkJZECYZdTq148","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"`test_large_sequence` fails because the test and the code disagree about what happens when the sequence number has more than four digits.\n\n- **The test** (`test_invoice.py:11`) expects `invoice_number(2026, 12345)` to return `\"INV-2026-2345\"`. That treats the sequence as a 4-digit field, so the leading `1` is dropped.\n- **The code** (`invoice.py:5`) uses `f\"INV-{year}-{seq:04}\"`. The `04` is a minimum width, so `12345` is printed in full: `\"INV-2026-12345\"`.\n\nI ran the module and got `AssertionError: 'INV-2026-12345' != 'INV-2026-2345'`. `test_format` passes.\n\nNeither side is clearly wrong, because the spec does not say what should happen past 9999. The fix depends on which behavior you want:\n\n1. **Keep the code, fix the test.** Overflow is shown in full, so `12345` stays `INV-2026-12345`. Change the expected value to `\"INV-2026-12345\"`. This is the safer choice, since silent truncation would make two different invoices share a number.\n2. **Keep the test, change the code.** Wrap the sequence to 4 digits with `seq % 10000`. Only do this if the number is meant to repeat every 10,000 invoices. It would also produce duplicate invoice numbers, which is usually a bug in billing.\n\nI have not changed either file. Which behavior do you want?"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":497,"cache_read_input_tokens":40911,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":497},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"524859ab-c5f6-426b-814a-f852c477523f","uuid":"2655a310-0136-4846-bf26-5fe1c3e10782","timestamp":"2026-10-27T